\chapter{MIR serialization, evaluation, and passes}
\label{ch:80}
\index{MIR serialization, evaluation, and passes}
\index{MIR!serialization}
\index{Optimization!MIR passes}

The text format serializes a versioned MIR profile and can be decoded and independently verified. The reference evaluator executes the admitted subset under bounds. The scalar pass pipeline includes constant folding, control-flow simplification, local-slot optimization and elimination of dead pure values. It works on private candidates and returns the last verified prefix when work or rewrite limits are reached.

\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none,language={}]
on1xc --emit-ir on1x/examples/mir.on1x -o build/input.mir
on1xc --run-ir --ir-input build/input.mir
on1xc --dump-passes --optimize-ir --ir-input build/input.mir
\end{OnxCodeBlock}

Compare evaluator outcomes before and after a rewrite, including a bounded trap case. Then compare round-trip text or deterministic output under the same profile. A pass removing unused arithmetic must still preserve a trap when that arithmetic can fault. MIR supplied from a file enters at MIR verification and bypasses the earlier source and HIR passes. Record whether the experiment begins with text or source when comparing pass logs.

\section{Decide when identical destinations mean identical edges}
Draw a conditional branch whose true and false successors are the same
block. Give that block one integer parameter. The true edge supplies 10
and the false edge supplies 20; the block prints its parameter. Even
though a graph viewer can draw both arrows to the same box, replacing
the branch by the first jump changes the output for a false condition.
The CFG simplifier compares complete argument vectors as well as destinations.
Change the false edge to supply the same value as the true edge: the
terminator can now become a jump without changing the delivered value.
The instruction that produced the condition may still have effects.

There is a related but different question for constant folding. Two
distinct SSA definitions can both be independently known integer literals
with value 10. If every incoming edge supplies such a value, the nonentry
parameter can become a constant in the destination, with the corresponding
argument removed from \emph{every} incoming edge. Knowing the literal on
just one edge cannot justify that edit. Nor can a numeric comparison of
two floating zeros substitute for comparing their exact signed bit patterns.

For a saved module, compare the verified text before and after and run
both condition values through the evaluator. Check the printed parameter,
not only the return status. When inspecting a pass report, remember that
removed IDs leave gaps: the number of surviving instructions is a count
of definitions, not the maximum numerical ID. A smaller verified graph
need not acquire a new numbering to be a successful rewrite. An effectful
call computing a no-longer-needed argument is still a root for dead-value
elimination: removing a parameter does not make its producer pure.

